  summary:"One full day at the retreat, and the discipline is doing less with it. The composed morning: the free riverside Morning Stretch at 08:30, then the in-room asa-nabe breakfast — the one paid treat of the day. The bamboo grove at dawn is the one excursion worth considering (ask the desk about the first boat or car down; about 1.5–2 hours door to door if you go straight there and back), and it is a choice, not a default. After that, choose: (A) stay put — garden, tea, reading, bath, nap; or (B) extend the morning loop to Tenryu-ji and Okochi Sanso instead and take the stretch off the schedule. At least half the day stays at the hotel either way. Mist or rain doesn't spoil today — it upgrades it.",
  about:"Arashiyama was the pleasure landscape of the Heian court — river, moon, blossom and season, appreciated from boats and villas rather than conquered on foot. HOSHINOYA translates that idea directly: boat-era access, framed views, gardens, incense and tea. The history lesson today isn't a monument; it's the experience of having urban friction removed. Ask the staff about the design of one ritual — the incense, the tea, the room's window — rather than consuming every activity on the sheet.", deeper:"<b>The bamboo grove is not a forest.</b> Moso bamboo is a grass, it grows up to a metre a day in season, and the grove is intensively managed — thinned, propped and harvested. The sound of it, which the Ministry of the Environment formally listed in 1996 as one of the hundred soundscapes of Japan to be preserved, only works when there is nobody in it. That is the reason for the 07:00 start.<br><br><b>On doing less.</b> The Japanese term for what this day is trying to do is <i>ma</i> — the interval, the deliberate emptiness between things, which in Japanese aesthetics is not absence but a component. It is the pause in a tea ceremony, the gap in a rock garden, the silence in gagaku court music. A day with nothing in it is not a wasted day here; it is the thing the rest of the trip is arranged around.",
