        history:"You're walking infrastructure from about 1600: the Tokugawa ordered the five highways standardised — width, milestone mounds (ichirizuka, one per ri, ~3.9 km), post stations — within a year of winning Sekigahara. The ishidatami stone paving underfoot was laid so daimyo processions and pack horses wouldn't slide on the wet climbs; the pass you cross (801 m) is why this leg was feared in winter. The tea house has stood at the midpoint since the Edo period — serving walkers was its entire economy, and in a sense still is.",
