        why:"Japan's first preserved townscape — residents signed a charter in 1971: 'don't sell, don't rent, don't destroy'. The Waki-Honjin Okuya (rebuilt 1877 in then-newly-legal hinoki cypress) is the interior the street only hints at: hearth smoke, light shafts, an emperor's bathroom built for a visit that lasted one rest stop.",
        history:"Tsumago was station 42, and its museums hold the road's best single story: when Princess Kazunomiya's 1861 bridal procession passed through, this tiny town had to lodge and feed thousands — the records of that logistical madness survive here. The Okuya was the waki-honjin (the 'overflow' official inn) run by the Hayashi family; Emperor Meiji stopped here in 1880, which is why a bathroom and toilet were built that were used exactly once. The 1971 residents' charter — don't sell, don't rent, don't destroy — has held for over fifty years, which may be the most impressive preservation fact in Japan.",
